  "entry": "E98",
  "article": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "gold_statement": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference",
  "gold_meaning": {
   "who": "DeepSeek",
   "what": "developing its own AI inference chip; underway about a year; early stage; in talks with chip designers, foundries and memory suppliers; no tape-out timeline",
   "status": "rumour"
  },
  "draws": {
   "A1": [
    "DeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.",
    "The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.",
    "In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] .",
    "It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself",
    "Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .",
    "Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] .",
    "OpenAI and Anthropic have both disclosed custom chip programs, while Google has long run its TPU chips for both training and inference. Amazon designs its Trainium and Inferentia chips for AWS customers [2] ."
   ],
   "A2": [
    "In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5].",
    "Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3].",
    "Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5]."
   ],
