 E28 The platform is now free for development, production, and redistribution, with no NVIDIA AI Enterprise subscription required {"who": "NVIDIA", "what": "Omniverse free for development, production and redistribution without an NVIDIA AI Enterprise subscription (previously $4,500 per GPU per year); paid enterprise support still sold through NVIDIA AI Enterprise", "period": "as of May 2026 (documentation); forum post 2026-07-01", "status": "happened"}
A2 ['The new terms remove that requirement entirely; anything built with Omniverse can also be redistributed under the same free terms, which is a substantive impact for ISVs and integrators who embed Omniverse applications inside their own products.']
A3 ['The new terms remove that requirement entirely; anything built with Omniverse can also be redistributed under the same free terms, which is a substantive impact for ISVs and integrators who embed Omniverse applications inside their own products.']
B1 ['Licensing documentation was updated to read “As of May 2026, Omniverse is freely available for both development and production use,” and a short announcement was posted to the NVIDIA Developer Forums on July 1.', 'In March, NVIDIA released the Omniverse DSX Blueprint alongside the Vera Rubin DSX AI factory reference design, a framework for building digital twins of gigawatt-scale GPU data centers. The blueprint unifies power, cooling, networking, and operations into a single simulated environment, with Cadence, Schneider Electric, Siemens, Vertiv, Eaton, Jacobs, and others contributing SimReady assets and integrating their design tools.', 'The first production deployment, the Mercedes-Benz CLA on the DRIVE platform, reached the US market this year.']
B2 ['Licensing documentation was updated to read “As of May 2026, Omniverse is freely available for both development and production use,” and a short announcement was posted to the NVIDIA Developer Forums on July 1.', 'The new terms remove that requirement entirely; anything built with Omniverse can also be redistributed under the same free terms, which is a substantive impact for ISVs and integrators who embed Omniverse applications inside their own products.', 'Organizations that want enterprise support SLAs still buy NVIDIA AI Enterprise through a reseller or CSP marketplace. Partners who redistribute Omniverse within commercial products can use an embedded licensing model in which the partner handles front-line support, and NVIDIA provides back-line support.', 'The first production deployment, the Mercedes-Benz CLA on the DRIVE platform, reached the US market this year.', 'In March, NVIDIA released the Omniverse DSX Blueprint alongside the Vera Rubin DSX AI factory reference design, a framework for building digital twins of gigawatt-scale GPU data centers.', 'The blueprint unifies power, cooling, networking, and operations into a single simulated environment, with Cadence, Schneider Electric, Siemens, Vertiv, Eaton, Jacobs, and others contributing SimReady assets and integrating their design tools.']
B3 ['Licensing documentation was updated to read “As of May 2026, Omniverse is freely available for both development and production use,” and a short announcement was posted to the NVIDIA Developer Forums on July 1.', 'The new terms remove that requirement entirely; anything built with Omniverse can also be redistributed under the same free terms, which is a substantive impact for ISVs and integrators who embed Omniverse applications inside their own products.', 'Partners who redistribute Omniverse within commercial products can use an embedded licensing model in which the partner handles front-line support, and NVIDIA provides back-line support.', 'The first production deployment, the Mercedes-Benz CLA on the DRIVE platform, reached the US market this year.', 'In March, NVIDIA released the Omniverse DSX Blueprint alongside the Vera Rubin DSX AI factory reference design, a framework for building digital twins of gigawatt-scale GPU data centers. The blueprint unifies power, cooling, networking, and operations into a single simulated environment, with Cadence, Schneider Electric, Siemens, Vertiv, Eaton, Jacobs, and others contributing SimReady assets and integrating their design tools.', 'BMW’s virtual factory work remains the canonical manufacturing example, and NVIDIA’s stated ambition is for every factory to have a digital twin before ground breaks.']

 E30 Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity {"who": "NVIDIA", "what": "'AI Compute Partnership': financial backstop and credit support for neocloud GPU deployments, renting back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, for standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on the supported capacity", "how_much": "revenue-share percentage not disclosed; NVIDIA declined details beyond the blog post", "status": "happened (launched 2026-07-01; Sharon AI and Firmus first adopters)"}
A1 ['Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the "AI Compute Partnership," under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers\' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .', 'Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .', "Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] ."]
A2 ['Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the "AI Compute Partnership," under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers\' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .', "Under the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] .", 'The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed, and Nvidia declined to offer further details beyond the blog post [3] .', "The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .", 'The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory and has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage infrastructure supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign cloud operations in Australia and the Asia-Pacific region [6] .']
A3 ['Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the "AI Compute Partnership," under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers\' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .', "Under the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] . In exchange, Nvidia earns both its standard product revenue from the hardware sale and a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity [1] .", "The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .", 'Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .', 'The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed, and Nvidia declined to offer further details beyond the blog post [3] .']
B1 ['Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the "AI Compute Partnership," under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers\' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1].', "Under the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3].", 'Nvidia described the model as providing "economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model" in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [1] [3].', 'Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2].', 'Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6].', 'The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory and has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage infrastructure supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign cloud operations in Australia and the Asia-Pacific region [6].', 'Prior to this formalization, Nvidia had provided demand guarantees and customer referrals to help neoclouds raise debt — CoreWeave secured $6.3 billion and Lambda $1.5 billion through arrangements in which Nvidia played a supporting role [1].']
